 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Inference is feed-forward and takes seconds (e.g., 2.9s on a single H800 GPU for 32 views) [S1, unofficial]. Training takes approximately 2-3 days on 8 NVIDIA H20 GPUs for 30,000 iterations [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Storage footprint is 35MB per scene, significantly lower than pixel-aligned methods (>650MB) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Gaussian Count:** Fixed at 0.26M Gaussians per scene, independent of input resolution and view count [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:**
-    *   **Sparse-View (3 views):** PSNR 23.69 on VR-NeRF, outperforming baselines like NoPoSplat and AnySplat [S1, unofficial].
-    *   **Dense-View (32 views):** PSNR 24.56 on VR-NeRF, comparable to state-of-the-art pixel-aligned baselines but with far fewer Gaussians [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Compact representation, fast inference, high quality in sparse-view settings, decoupling of Gaussian count from input views.
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on geometry backbone quality, fixed anchor budget may not scale to very large scenes, requires pre-trained VGGT weights [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** When fast, feed-forward novel view synthesis is needed, especially in sparse-view settings, or when storage/compute constraints are tight.
-*   **When Not to Use:** For very large or unbounded scenes where a fixed anchor budget is insufficient, or when high-quality geometry backbone predictions are not available.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Inference is feed-forward and takes seconds (e.g., 2.9s on H800 for VR-NeRF), compared to minutes for per-scene optimization methods like VGGT+3DGS [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Storage is compact. 0.26M Gaussians per scene require ~35MB, compared to >650MB for pixel-aligned methods producing several million Gaussians [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:**
+    *   **Sparse-View (3 views) on VR-NeRF:** PSNR 23.69 (Full model) vs 22.50 (w/o enhancement), 23.03 (w/o self-attention), 23.28 (w/o occ-aggregation), 22.16 (w/o entropy-sample) [S1, unofficial].
+    *   **Dense-View (32 views) on VR-NeRF:** PSNR 24.56, SSIM 0.815, LPIPS 0.297 [S1, unofficial].
+    *   **Dense-View (32 views) on DL3DV:** PSNR 19.52, SSIM 0.593, LPIPS 0.364 [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Scaling:** Gaussian count (0.26M) is independent of input resolution and view count, unlike pixel-aligned methods where count scales with views/resolution [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** State-of-the-art in sparse-view settings; order of magnitude fewer Gaussians and lower storage in dense-view settings; fast feed-forward inference; mitigates holes in poorly observed regions.
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on geometry backbone accuracy; fixed anchor budget may not suit very large/unbounded scenes; refinement UNet adds some computational overhead compared to raw splatting.
+*   **When to Use:** When fast, pose-free novel view synthesis is required with limited storage/compute, especially in sparse-view scenarios.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When per-scene optimization is acceptable for higher quality, or when scenes are extremely large and require adaptive anchor budgets.
 
 ## Background
-Anchor3DGS was introduced in the paper "Anchor3DGS: Feed-Forward 3D Gaussian Splatting with Compact Anchor-Based Representation" [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the limitations of pixel-aligned 3DGS methods, which produce a large number of Gaussians proportional to input resolution and view count, leading to high storage and compute costs. By introducing a compact anchor-based representation, Anchor3DGS achieves state-of-the-art quality in sparse-view settings and comparable quality in dense-view settings with significantly fewer Gaussians and lower storage. It is evaluated on VR-NeRF, MipNeRF360, and DL3DV datasets [S1, unofficial].
+Anchor3DGS was introduced in the paper "Anchor3DGS: Feed-Forward 3D Gaussian Splatting with Compact Anchor-Based Representation" [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the limitations of pixel-aligned 3DGS methods, which produce large numbers of Gaussians proportional to input views and require per-scene optimization. By adopting an anchor-based paradigm, it enables pose-free, feed-forward reconstruction with compact representation. Evaluated on VR-NeRF, MipNeRF360, and DL3DV datasets [S1, unofficial].
 
